Here’s an optimized JavaScript regex pattern for validating standard email addresses, designed to perform better on low-end mobile devices:

```javascript
const validateEmail = (email) => {
  // Optimized regex for standard email validation
  const re = /^[\w-]+(\.[\w-]+)*@[a-zA-Z0-9-]+(\.[a-zA-Z0-9-]+)*$/;
  return re.test(email);
};
```

### Explanation of the Optimized Regex:
1. **`^` and `$` Anchors**: These ensure the entire string is matched from start to end, avoiding partial matches.
2. **`[\w-]+`**: Matches one or more word characters (letters, digits, and underscores) or hyphens. This is stricter than the broader `[a-zA-Z0-9.!#$%&'*+/=?^_`{|}~-]` set, focusing on common cases.
